<p>The <strong>Geoneo under desk elliptical</strong> is a compact manual seated elliptical: you sit in your normal chair, rest your feet on two pedals and move them in a smooth oval against resistance you set with a dial. It is one of the more popular budget names in the category, mostly with home office workers who want to move while they work.</p>
<p>Our verdict up front: the manual Geoneo is a solid value choice for light to moderate daily use. It does not match the quietness, smoothness or support of the Cubii JR2, which is the model we compare it with in detail below. If you can live with a slightly rougher ride, the Geoneo saves you money and does the core job well.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Quick verdict:</strong> pick the Geoneo for budget-friendly seated movement. Pick the [[amazon:cubii-jr2]] if you take lots of calls, want the smoothest ride, or plan to use it every day for years.</p></div>

<h2>What Is the Geoneo Under Desk Elliptical Machine?</h2>
<p>The Geoneo under desk elliptical machine follows the standard compact design: a weighted flywheel inside a housing, two crank arms with textured foot platforms, a resistance knob and a small display. There is no seat, handles or upright frame. It sits on the floor in front of whatever chair you already use.</p>
<p>The display tracks the basics, typically time, strides or distance, and an estimated calorie count. Treat the calorie number as a rough indicator rather than a measurement, since it cannot know your weight or effort.</p>
<h3>Geoneo elliptical vs "Geoneo exercise bike"</h3>
<p>Many shoppers call this machine an exercise bike, and some search for a "Geoneo exercise bike" when they mean the elliptical. The difference matters. A desk bike moves your feet in a circle, which bends the knee more at the top of each turn. An elliptical keeps your feet flat on platforms and moves them in an oval, which most people find gentler on knees and easier to do while typing. Our <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-bike/">under desk elliptical vs bike</a> guide explains which suits you.</p>
<h3>Manual vs auto modes</h3>
